          "text": "{\"regions\":{\"r2\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"indeclinable ( \u0938\u094d\u0935\u0930\u0935\u094d\u092f\u092f\u0902\u2014Amara. ) and has generally the sense\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"of the acc. or loc. It is compounded with certain nouns as,\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"\u0938\u094d\u0935\u0930\u094d\u0917\u0947, \u0938\u094d\u0935\u0930\u094d\u0923\u0926\u0940, \u0938\u094d\u0935\u0930\u093e\u091f\u094d ( \u0938\u094d\u0935\u0903 + \u0930\u093e\u091f\u094d ) &c. \u0927\u0943\u0937\u094d\u091f\u0903\u2014impudent, shameless\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"( \u0928\u093f\u0930\u094d\u0932\u091c\u094d\u091c ). The fig. of speech is \u0935\u093f\u092d\u093e\u0935\u0928\u093e.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"Some Sanskrit compounds are not fully clear.\"},\"r3\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 11. \u0928\u093e\u092e\u2014Verily, forsooth; or \u2018 may well be considered\u2019.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"\u0917\u094d\u0930\u093e\u0939\u2014A shark. \u0935\u093f\u0924\u0930\u094d\u0915\u2014misgivings, doubts; \u0905\u0938\u094d\u092e\u093f\u0928\u094d\u0915\u0943\u0924\u0947 \u0907\u0926\u0902 \u092d\u0935\u093f\u0937\u094d\u092f\u0924\u093f\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"\u0928 \u092d\u0935\u093f\u0937\u094d\u092f\u0924\u0940\u0924\u093f \u091a\u0947\u0924\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0930\u0942\u092a\u093e\u0903 \u090a\u0939\u093e\u0903 R\u00e1marshi. \u0927\u0948\u0930\u094d\u092f\u0926\u094d\u0930\u0941\u092e\u0927\u094d\u0935\u0902\u0938\u093f\u0928\u0940\u2014felling the\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"tree of courage on its bank. Cf. infra. \u0938\u093f\u0915\u0924\u093f\u0932\u0928\u0926\u0940\u0924\u0940\u0930\u0924\u0930\u0941\u092d\u093f\u0903 Sl. 49.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"\u0906\u0935\u0930\u094d\u0924\u2014an eddy, a whirlpool. \u0924\u091f\u0940\u2014the bank of a river. The\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"comp. should properly be \u0924\u091f\u0940\u0915\u093e; but it may be defended by the\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"maxim \u0938\u092e\u093e\u0938\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0935\u093f\u0927\u093f\u0930\u0928\u093f\u0924\u094d\u092f\u0903. \u0924\u0938\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0903 \u092a\u093e\u0930\u0917\u0924\u093e\u0903\u2014those that have\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"crossed it ( the river ) i. e. have conquered desire.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"Several Sanskrit words are indistinct.\"},\"r4\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 12. \u0938\u0902\u0938\u093e\u0930\u094b\u0924\u094d\u092a\u0928\u094d\u0928\u0902 \u091a\u0930\u093f\u0924\u0902\u2014The course of life in this world;\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"incidents of worldly life. \u0905\u0928\u0941\u092a\u0936\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092e\u093f\u2014to expect something good\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"out of, to see in prospect; cf. \u0928 \u091a \u0936\u094d\u0930\u0947\u092f\u094b\u093d\u0928\u0941\u092a\u0936\u094d\u092f\u093e\u092e\u093f Bhag. I. 31. \u0935\u093f\u092a\u093e\u0915\u0903\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":4,\"text\":\"\u2014result. \u0935\u093f\u091a\u093f\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0924\u0903\u2014to me when I reflect, when I take a right\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":5,\"text\":\"view of the matter. See Ni. S\u2019a. 89. \u091a\u093f\u0930\u092a\u0930\u093f\u0917\u0943\u0939\u0940\u0924\u093e\u0903\u2014( 1 ) enjoyed\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":6,\"text\":\"for a long time, or (2) enjoyed after a long time, as R\u00e1marshi\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":7,\"text\":\"takes it. The former is, however, preferable, as long enjoyment\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":8,\"text\":\"is required to make a man attached to worldly pleasures which\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":9,\"text\":\"is implied in the succeeding line. \u092e\u0939\u093e\u0928\u094d\u0924\u094b \u091c\u093e\u092f\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0947\u2014become big;\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":10,\"text\":\"assume large proportions; hence tempt men by their magnitude.\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":11,\"text\":\"\u0935\u094d\u092f\u0938\u0928\u0902 \u0926\u093e\u0924\u0941\u092e\u093f\u0935\u2014their object in assuming big proportions and\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":12,\"text\":\"tempting men away from Moksha being as it were to make\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":13,\"text\":\"men unhappy. The more a man clings to pleasures the more\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":14,\"text\":\"miserable he becomes; for he is always disappointed; cf. \u0928 \u091c\u093e\u0924\u0941\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":15,\"text\":\"\u0915\u093e\u092e\u0903 \u0915\u093e\u092e\u093e\u0928\u093e\u092e\u0941\u092a\u092d\u094b\u0917\u0947\u0928 \u0936\u093e\u092e\u094d\u092f\u0924\u093f \u0964 \u0939\u0935\u093f\u0937\u093e \u0915\u0943\u0937\u094d\u0923\u0935\u0930\u094d\u0924\u094d\u092e\u0947\u0935 \u092d\u0942\u092f \u090f\u0935\u093e\u092d\u093f\u0935\u0930\u094d\u0927\u0924\u0947 \u0965 Manu\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":16,\"text\":\"II. 94. Or \u0935\u094d\u092f\u0938\u0928 may mean calamity; the fondness for enjoy-\",\"end\":\"hyphen\"},{\"n\":17,\"text\":\"ments throws them into the turmoil of embodied existence; see\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":18,\"text\":\"com. and infra. Sl. 46. notes. \u0935\u093f\u0937\u092f\u093f\u0923\u093e\u0902\u2014and not to \u0935\u093f\u0930\u093e\u0917\u093f\u0923\u093e\u0902 who at\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":19,\"text\":\"once renounce them and become happy.\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"Some Sanskrit words, particularly the opening phrase, are difficult to read.\"},\"r5\":{\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"Sl. 13. \u091a\u093f\u0930\u0924\u0930\u092e\u0941\u0937\u093f\u0924\u094d\u0935\u093e\u092a\u093f\u2014even after staying long. \u0935\u093f\u092f\u094b\u0917\u0947\u2014is\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":2,\"text\":\"here used in the sense of \u2018the vanishing of pleasures,\u2019 and is\",\"end\":\"space\"},{\"n\":3,\"text\":\"explained by the words \u0935\u094d\u0930\u091c\u0928\u094d\u0924\u0903 \u0938\u094d\u0935\u093e\u0924\u0928\u094d\u0924\u094d\u0930\u094d\u092f\u093e\u0924\u094d below. \u0938\u094d\u0935\u092f\u092e\u094d\u2014of one\u2019s\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":true,\"note\":\"The first Sanskrit compound is indistinct.\"},\"r1\":{\"type\":\"page_number\",\"lines\":[{\"n\":1,\"text\":\"( 54 )\",\"end\":\"space\"}],\"uncertain\":false,\"note\":null}},\"units\":[{\"id\":\"u1\",\"type\":\"notes\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"u2\",\"u3\",\"u4\",\"u5\"]},{\"id\":\"u2\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":null,\"label\":null,\"open_start\":true,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r2\"]},{\"id\":\"u3\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"11\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r3\"]},{\"id\":\"u4\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"12\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":false,\"children\":[\"r4\"]},{\"id\":\"u5\",\"type\":\"stanza_note\",\"number\":\"13\",\"label\":null,\"open_start\":false,\"open_end\":true,\"children\":[\"r5\"]}],\"flows\":[{\"flow\":\"main\",\"units\":[\"u1\"]}],\"relations\":[]}"
        }
